<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frauenkirche, Munich</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1468">1468</a>–<a href="/years/1488">1488</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Cathedral of Our Lady, Munich</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Frauenkirche is the cathedral of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archdiocese-of-munich-and-freising">Archdiocese of Munich and Freising</a>. Built in brick between 1468 and 1488 under Duke Sigismund and then Duke Albert IV, to designs by Jörg von Halsbach, it was consecrated in 1494. Its twin towers with rounded domes, completed in 1525, are the best-known landmark of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, and it holds the tomb of Emperor Louis IV.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frauenkirche-munich.jpg" alt="Frauenkirche, Munich" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diliff · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frauenkirche_Munich_-_View_from_Peterskirche_Tower2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>A late Romanesque church stood beside the town's first ring of walls from the 12th century and served as the second city parish after the older Alter Peter. The late Gothic cathedral that replaced it was commissioned by Duke Sigismund and the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> in the 15th century and raised in about twenty years by Jörg von Halsbach. Because no stone quarry lay nearby, and for financial reasons, brick was chosen. Construction began in 1468, and when the money ran out in 1479 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a> granted an indulgence. The church was consecrated in 1494. Its size was striking: it could hold 20,000 standing people in a city of about 13,000 inhabitants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The church is of red brick, designed very plainly, with its buttresses moved into the interior. It is 109 metres long, 40 metres wide and 37 metres high, a hall church whose nave and two aisles are of equal height and are divided by a double row of 22 pillars. Heinrich von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/straubing">Straubing</a> designed the arches. The twin towers stand just over 98 metres high; the north tower is 98.57 metres and the south 98.45, though a legend says they differ by one metre. Plans for tall open-work spires were never carried out for lack of money, and in 1525 the towers received domes in a cheaper design, possibly modelled on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock">Dome of the Rock</a>.</p><p>Inside are the tomb monument of Emperor Louis IV, Gothic stained glass and choir stalls carved by Erasmus Grasser between 1495 and 1502. The stalls burned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, but their figures had been moved and survive. The crypt holds the tombs of the Archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freising">Freising</a> and of members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">Wittelsbach dynasty</a>. A black mark at the entrance, the Devil's Footstep, is linked by legend to the devil, who stood there and mocked the church for seeming to have no windows.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In 1919 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eugen-levine">Eugen Leviné</a>, leader of a short-lived Bavarian Socialist Republic, had the church declared a revolutionary temple. In the later stages of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> air raids collapsed the roof, badly damaged a tower and destroyed or lost most of its historical contents. A restoration in several stages began soon after the war and was completed in 1994. Its domed towers have made it a symbol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frauenkirche, Munich” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Albert IV, duke from 1465, built a stronger, more centralised state, and Munich's <b>Frauenkirche</b> was completed under his rule in 1488.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bavaria-munich">Duchy of Bavaria-Munich</a></li></ul></section>
